20610 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Kotlin down the years\054 and its role today\nKotlin\054 the programming language developed by JetBrains\054 is no longer young \023 it\031s mature\056\050Fifteen years from initial release\054 it\031s reached an estimated 8\0561 million developers worldwide\056 Since Google\031s official support for Kotlin on Android in 2017\054 the language has demonstrated sustained rather than cyclical growth\054 attracting developers across new domains while continuing to strengthen its presence in existing ones\056 Rather than peaking in a single area\054 Kotlin has steadily expanded across Android\054 backend\054 multiplatform\054 and AI\055related development\056 Here\031s a summary of our findings\056\nSteady growth\054 confirmed by outside observers\056 Independent rankings tell the same story as JetBrains\031 own data\056 Kotlin was among the top five fastest\055growing languages on GitHub in 2024\054 is among the most admired languages on Stack Overflow\054 and has held a\240stable position in RedMonk rankings since 2024\056 All this data suggests it holds a durable and sustainable market position\056 Section 1 ahead explores it further\056\050\nOrganizations adopt Kotlin incrementally\054 without rip\055and\055replace\056 Kotlin is in play at firms of every size and across many industries\054 with around 80\045 of developers using it in production\056 Interoperability is the enabler\072 Over two\055thirds of Kotlin developers used Java as their primary language before\054 giving Kotlin a clear route into environments without a disruptive migration\056 Section 2 examines how it enters and spreads through today\031s organizations\056\050\nThe talent pool is maturing as well as expanding\056 Two\055thirds of Kotlin developers have more than two years of experience\054 while nearly one in five started using the language within the past year\054 indicating a steady influx of new developers alongside an established community\056 Java remains the dominant feeder pool \050which lowers hiring risk\051\054 but the ecosystem is now producing Kotlin\055first developers without prior Java backgrounds\056 \050This workforce is profiled in Section 3\056\050\nKotlin Multiplatform \050KMP\051 has evolved from an experimental technology into a\240production\055ready platform\054 and its adoption is accelerating in both ecosystem surveys and real\055world usage\056 KMP\047s share among cross\055platform developers more than doubled in a single year\054 and its presence among the top 10\054000 App Store apps nearly tripled between early 2024 and mid\0552026\054 growing faster than Flutter and React Native\056 \050Adoption also runs deep\072 Roughly 60\045 of KMP projects share all or almost all of their business logic across platforms\056 Section 4 sets out how teams use KMP\056\050\nBackend development is now a core use case\056 Around half of Kotlin developers work on backend projects\054 and in JetBrains\047 anonymized IDE data\054 almost nine in ten large engineering organizations already run some Kotlin on the backend\056 Development cycles are 15\045\02320\045 shorter than in comparable Java work\054 and the ecosystem now carries enterprise weight \023 a strategic partnership with Spring and a formal security and support policy\056 Section 5 goes deeper into stability\054 productivity\054 and adoption paths\056\050\nKotlin\047s strengths carry over to the AI era\056 Nine out of ten Kotlin developers use AI coding tools\054 and the language works in their favor\072 When a model invents a method or misuses a\240nullable value\054 the code simply fails to compile\054 surfacing mistakes before anything runs\056 Developers report most AI\055generated Kotlin works after minor fixes\054 and a production AI\240stack has emerged \023 Spring AI\054 Koog\054 and the Kotlin MCP SDK\056 Section 6 explores these developments\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 20611 0 R
     /Pg 1774 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

