21361 0 obj
  << /ActualText (In any organization of meaningful size\054 Kotlin almost always arrives into an existing Java codebase rather than being there from the start\056 The Java systems continue to run untouched\054 and the two languages coexist long\055term\054 as most Kotlin backend developers still write Java\054 as well\056\nThe most common unit of adoption is a whole service\056 A team builds its next service in Kotlin\054 and that service is essentially all\055Kotlin from day one\054 while the services around it stay Java\056 Roughly three\055quarters of backend codebases that contain any Kotlin are almost entirely Kotlin\056 The pattern is the same in every country we observed\054 from the most Kotlin\055heavy markets to those where adoption is just beginning\056\nThe classic entry paths experts describe are the small\055scale version of the same motion \050\023 Kotlin enters next to working Java\054 in a bounded piece\054 without touching the rest\072\nTests first\056 The first Kotlin in many codebases is JUnit test code\072 low\055risk\054 easy to review\054 and immediately less verbose\056\050\nOne module at a time\056 A new module is written in Kotlin and runs in the existing codebase\054 next to working Java code\054 with the same Jackson serialization and JPA persistence\056\050\nA new Spring Boot service\056 The most common first Kotlin project is a service on the framework the team already knows\056\nThere is also a measurable payoff that keeps the expansion going once the first service ships\056 JetBrains\031 2026 research analyzed IDE data from over 300\054000 software developers and found Kotlin development cycles to be 15\045\02320\045 shorter than comparable Java cycles for everyday tasks\056 The reason isn\031t just faster coding\056 According to JetBrains\031 own observations\054 as projects grow more complex\054 Java\031s cycle times tend to climb\054 while Kotlin\031s rise more gradually\056\nFor decision makers\054 the combination is practical\072 Kotlin can be introduced without replacing the JVM stack\054 and its effect can be tested in a controlled pilot before broader adoption\056 Hiring reflects the same reality \023 Kotlin backend roles are typically advertised as \042Java\057Kotlin\042\054 so the talent pool stays the JVM talent pool\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 21362 0 R
     /Pg 12629 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

