21453 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Java teams add Kotlin at modernization moments\056 Projects that went through the Spring Boot 2\2223 migration were eight times more likely to add Kotlin than those that didn\047t upgrade\054 and about seven in ten new Kotlin backend adopters already had Spring in the project when Kotlin arrived\056 Teams revisit their language choice when they are already reworking the codebase\054 not on an ordinary Tuesday\056\nThe teams most likely to take that step are those that proactively maintain their tech stacks\056 For instance\054 teams running recent JDKs add Kotlin at a significantly higher rate than those stuck on JDK 8\056 At first glance\054 that might seem backward \023 if a team has already modernized its Java\054 hasn\047t modern Java met its needs\077 The data says the opposite\056 Upgrading the JDK is not a finish line\073 it marks a team that treats modernization as a habit\056 The same habit that took a team to the latest JDK takes it to Kotlin at the next opportunity\056 Kotlin adoption is not a break from the Java roadmap \023 it is what actively modernizing JVM teams tend to do next\056\nThat makes the present moment notable\072 The Spring Boot 3 line reached open\055source \050end\055of\055life in June 2026\054 and the ecosystem is moving to Spring Boot 4\056 For teams planning that migration\054 the record suggests this is exactly when the language question opens \050\023 and a new or rewritten service is the easiest place to answer it with Kotlin\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 21454 0 R
     /Pg 13975 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

