21629 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Use of AI tools has become standard practice among Kotlin developers\056 Among Kotlin users who took part in the 2025 JetBrains Developer Ecosystem Survey\054 AI tools had already become part of regular development practice\072 Over 90\045 regularly use at least one AI coding tool\054 and four in five already use or are likely to try AI coding agents that carry out multistep tasks rather than generate single snippets\056\nCompany adoption is broad but measured\056 Nearly every organization is doing something \050with AI\072 A third have reached partial adoption\054 most of the rest are piloting or still exploring\054 and a smaller group has taken AI into extensive use\056 The pattern is high but gradual engagement \023 scaling what works rather than leaping into the unknown\056\nKotlin holds a useful advantage here\056 Its static typing and null safety work as guardrails on \050AI\055generated code\056 When a model invents a method or misuses a nullable value\054 the code fails to compile \023 and modern agents use that signal\054 building the project and reading compiler errors to fix their own output before presenting a diff\056 The compiler cannot catch flawed logic that passes the type checker\073 developers still review what they ship\056 Some type and nullability errors are surfaced mechanically\054 giving an agent an opportunity to correct them before presenting a final change\054 which is what makes AI output in a statically typed language cheaper to review\056\240\nFor a decision\055maker\054 the takeaway is narrower but real\056 Kotlin doesn\047t remove the need \050to review AI output \023 nothing does \023 but it catches a class of failures mechanically and makes each review cheaper\056 The next section shows how developers handle the rest\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 21630 0 R
     /Pg 16390 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

